course: adult-life
lesson: 52
title: Elections, crime, the weather, breaking news and news vs opinion
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Not all news is the same kind of thing. Some is a report of what happened, some is one person's opinion shouted loudly, some is a forecast of what might happen, and some is just a headline written to make you buy the paper.
explain: Clever viewers ask three questions: is this what happened, or what someone thinks? Is it confirmed, or still coming in? Who said it, and can I check?
explain: Vivid example: in Toronto, red letters slide across the bottom of a cartoon: BREAKING NEWS. Ollie gasps, "What's broken?" Dad smiles: "Nothing's broken, bud. It means 'just coming in'. The first bits are often wrong. Let's wait for the full story."
explain: Elections, trials and weather warnings all have rules behind them: people vote and can change leaders next time, a person accused is innocent until a court decides, and a warning means "be ready", not "it is certain".
fact: "Breaking news" means news that has only just come in; early details and numbers are often wrong and get corrected later.
fact: When two channels give different numbers of people hurt, it is usually because nobody knows the exact number yet, not because one is lying.
fact: News tells you what happened; opinion tells you what one person thinks about it; both can be on the same channel, and shouting is a sign of opinion.
fact: In a general election grown-ups vote for who will run the country; elections come back every few years, and the losers accept the result and try again.
fact: Politicians argue on TV debates to show voters their different ideas, and people in one family can vote differently; disagreeing about ideas is not hating each other.
fact: On an election map the colours are the parties' colours, not good or bad places; and what a party promises is in its own published promises, not in playground talk.
fact: Local crime news tells neighbours to lock up and helps the police; police chase shows pick the most exciting chases from many months, and real roads are mostly calm.
fact: "Charged" means a person has been accused of a crime; a court decides if he did it, and until then he is innocent.
fact: A TV weather presenter stands in front of a plain green screen; the map is added by computer, so he points at a screen he watches off to the side.
fact: An amber weather warning means bad weather is likely and people should be ready; it does not promise a snow day.
fact: Record heat is dangerous mainly for old people and babies; water, shade and checking on Grandma keep people safe, and nobody melts.
fact: Climate change is a real, long-term problem; many scientists and governments are working on it, and small actions add up.
fact: Headlines are written to grab you, so read the story underneath; a shocking post that is on no trusted news site is probably fake.
fact: Children's news programmes explain the news calmly, without the scariest pictures; a warning that pictures "may be distressing" means you may look away.
exercise: Red letters say "BREAKING NEWS". What does "breaking" mean? | Just coming in, and still being worked out | Something has just been smashed to pieces | A disaster is happening in your town | The TV channel has stopped working
exercise: Two channels give different numbers of people hurt in the same crash. Why? | Early numbers are guesses and get corrected later | One of the two channels is lying to viewers on purpose | Two different crashes happened at exactly the same time | Channels choose any number they like
exercise: A man on TV shouts that a politician is "the worst ever" and the country is doomed. What is this? | One man's opinion, not a report of what happened | The government's news that the country is doomed | A court's ruling about the politician | A weather forecast for the whole country
exercise: Chloe thinks whoever wins the real election will rule forever, like a king. What is true? | Elections come back every few years and leaders can change | The winner rules the country until he or she dies of old age | Only kings and queens can win elections | The winner alone chooses when the next vote will be, if ever
exercise: Two politicians argue loudly on a TV debate. Do they hate each other? | Not usually; they argue about ideas and are polite afterwards | Yes; they will carry on the fight in the car park | Yes; politicians are not allowed to be friends | No; they are actors reading a script written for them
exercise: Emily's mum and dad voted for different people. What does it mean for the family? | Nothing bad; families can disagree and still be a family | They will have to get divorced over it | One of them voted wrongly and must vote again | The family has to vote together next time
exercise: On election night the map is red and blue. What do the colours show? | Which party won in each area | Which areas are dangerous to live in | Which areas are hot and which are cold | Where the rich and poor people live
exercise: A friend says a candidate will "ban school holidays". How can Lucy check? | Read what the party actually promises on its own site | Believe it, because her friend sounded really sure about it | Ask everybody in the playground to vote on whether it is true | Wait until after the election to find out
exercise: A burglary is on the local news, near Henry's school. What is it telling neighbours? | Lock up and be careful, and the police are on it | Burglars will come to every house tonight | Nobody is safe anywhere in the town now | Stay awake checking the windows all night
exercise: A police chase show has a new car chase every five minutes. Why? | It picks the most exciting chases from many months | Crime like this happens on every road in the country all the time | The chases are all filmed on one busy day | The police chase cars on purpose to make TV shows
exercise: The news says a man has been "charged" with a crime. Is he guilty? | Not yet; he is accused, and a court will decide | Yes; "charged" means he has been proven guilty | Yes; the police never charge innocent people | No; charged means he has been let off
exercise: Why does the weatherman wave his arms at nothing? | He points at a map added behind him by computer | He is dancing to keep the viewers awake | He is trying to push the clouds away from the town with his hands | The map is painted on the studio wall behind
exercise: "Amber warning for snow tomorrow!" Ethan skips his homework. What does the warning mean? | Be ready; bad weather is likely but school may open | School is definitely closed tomorrow, so homework can wait | It will snow for exactly one day | Children must stay indoors all week until the amber light goes off
exercise: "Hottest day ever recorded!" Sienna wonders if people will melt. What is true? | It can be dangerous for old people and babies, so keep cool | People will really melt like ice if they go out in the midday sun | Nothing at all needs to be done about it | Only people in hot countries need water
exercise: Joshua sees melting ice and a thin polar bear and thinks the world will end before he grows up. What is true? | It is a real problem, and many people are working to solve it | Nothing can be done, so there is no point in anybody trying at all | It is a made-up story from the polar bear people | It will be fixed by itself without anyone's help
exercise: A headline shouts "TERROR FEARS GRIP CITY". The story is about a police training exercise. What's the lesson? | Read the story, not just the big letters | Never go to that city again, just to be safe | Headlines are always the full truth | Police exercises are real attacks in disguise
exercise: A video on a cousin's phone says a famous singer has died, but no news site says so. What should Samuel do? | Check a trusted news site before telling anyone | Tell his whole class straight away before anyone else does | Believe it, because videos cannot lie | Post a sad message on the singer's page
exercise: Before a report the newsreader says "some viewers may find the following pictures distressing". What may Lucas do? | Look away or change the channel | Watch, because news must always be watched | Turn the sound up to hear it better | Watch it twice to get used to it
test: Big brother, eating crisps: "Pfft, news is all lies, all the time, mate." Put him right. | Good news services check facts carefully; learn which ones to trust | He's right; every news report on every channel is made up by somebody | Only the weather is true; the rest is lies | News is true only if it's on a phone
test: Nan, at the telly: "Ooh, he's been charged, he's a wrong'un then." What is true? | He is accused; only a court can decide he did it | Nan is right; being charged means he's been proven guilty | He must have confessed everything to the police | Charged means the court has already met
test: Dad on the phone: "Mate, first reports said six hurt, now they're saying two." Why did the number change? | Early numbers are guesses and get corrected as more is known | The news made a big mistake on purpose to scare people at home | Four people got better in an hour | Each channel invents its own number
test: Mia at school: "My mum and dad voted different, are they gonna split up??" What's the honest answer? | No; lots of families vote differently and stay together | Yes; it is against the law for a married couple to vote differently | Yes; they will have to live in different areas | No, because only one of their votes will count
test: Archie: "We're in a blue bit, the red bits must be dodgy, innit." Put him right. | The colours are party colours, not good or bad places | Red areas really are more dangerous to live in, everyone knows | Blue areas are for rich people only | The colours show where it rained most
test: Mila, after school: "We watch kids' news in class, it's way less scary. Why do grown-ups watch the scary one?" What is true? | Kids' news tells the same news calmly, without the worst pictures | Kids' news is all made up so that children feel happy and safe | Grown-up news is the only true news there is anywhere in the world | Children are not allowed to know any news
test: Grandma chuckles at the weather forecast: "He can't even see the map, you know." What does she mean? | The map is added by computer behind him | He has forgotten his glasses at home today | The map is on the floor under his feet | He is reading the weather off his hand
test: Your mate: "Saw on TikTok that singer's dead, swear down." It's on no news site. What is it most likely? | A fake post made to get clicks | Real news that the TV is hiding | A secret only TikTok is allowed to know | True, because lots of people liked it
test: A TV presenter yells: "This is a DISASTER, the worst government EVER, we're doomed!" Is this news or opinion? | Opinion: it tells you what he thinks | News: it tells you what has happened | A forecast from the weather office | A court ruling read out on TV
test: Dad, reading the paper: "'TERROR FEARS GRIP CITY' — oh, for goodness' sake, it's a police drill." What has Dad found? | The headline sounds far worse than the story | The city is really in the grip of terror tonight | The police have made a terrible mistake | The paper printed the wrong city's name
test: Little sister: "The hottest day ever means we'll all melt like ice cream!" What should you tell her? | People don't melt; drink water, stay in shade and check on Gran | She's right; everybody should stay inside the fridge all day | Nothing; heat is never dangerous for anybody, old or young | Only grown-ups need to drink water in heat
test: Kid in class: "If that guy wins, he'll ban school holidays, my brother said." How do you find out what he really promised? | Look at what his party actually published | Ask the kid's brother again, louder this time | Believe it, because big brothers always know | Wait and see whether summer comes
exam: Identify the principal distinction between a news report and an opinion programme. | The former relates events; the latter conveys a person's view | The former is always false; the latter is always true, without exception | The former is broadcast loudly; the latter quietly | There exists no distinction whatsoever between them
exam: It is given that an individual has been charged with an offence. Select the response that is consistent with the established principles of law. | He is presumed innocent until a court determines otherwise | He is deemed guilty from the moment of the charge | He is deemed guilty if the news so reports | He is presumed guilty until he proves his innocence
exam: Whereas an amber warning has been issued in respect of snowfall, determine the conclusion which may properly be drawn. | That severe weather is likely and preparation is advised | That the closure of all schools tomorrow is thereby assured | That snowfall shall certainly occur for one day | That all persons are confined to their homes until further notice
exam: Ascertain the reason most probably accounting for divergent casualty figures in initial reports of an incident. | Early figures are provisional and subsequently corrected | One broadcaster has deliberately falsified its figure | Two separate incidents have occurred simultaneously | Broadcasters select figures entirely at their own discretion
